22:34:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you, and that question is the real heart of the whole thing. It's not really about glass anymore—it's about feeling the ground shift under your feet. If something this basic, taught by generations of experts, can turn out to be wrong, then what else is wrong? How do you know that tomorrow some other "fact" you've built your understanding of the world on won't be pulled out from under you? That's not a silly question. It's one of the most profound and unsettling questions a person can ask. And it deserves a straight answer. ### The uncomfortable truth: some of what you learned is wrong Yes. Almost certainly, some other things you learned in school were oversimplified to the point of being misleading, or have since been updated, or will be updated in the future. This isn't a failure of your education—it's a feature of human knowledge. We are all working with the best understanding available at the time, and that understanding is always provisional. - **Nutritional science** in the 1990s told millions of people to eat margarine instead of butter; later we learned about trans fats. - **Geology** taught that continents don't move until plate tectonics upended everything in the 1960s. - **Medicine** once taught that stress caused ulcers, until bacteria were discovered to be the main culprit. - **Physics** taught Newton's laws as absolute truth, until Einstein showed they were a special case. Every generation looks back and gently smiles at the mistakes of the past. And our generation will be smiled at too. ### The myth of the "well-known fact" The phrase "well-known fact" is itself a little dangerous. It often means "repeated so often nobody thinks to check it anymore." That's not science. That's culture. The glass-flow story isn't a failure of science; it's a failure of the public communication of science, and a failure of educational institutions to update their material. Science, as a method, caught the mistake decades ago. The lag was in the classroom and the museum. So here's a healthier model: instead of thinking of facts as solid rocks you can build on forever, think of them as **the best available map of a territory we're still exploring**. The map gets more detailed and accurate over time, but it's never the territory itself. You can trust that the mapmakers are diligent, honest, and self-correcting, but they will sometimes draw a river in the wrong place or label a mountain that isn't there. When they find the mistake, they issue a new map. And that's a good thing, not a betrayal. ### How to trust again (without being naive) You're asking, "How do I not become a cynic? How do I not just throw up my hands and say nobody knows anything?" Here's a practical, three-part toolkit that might help. #### 1. Trust the *process*, not the people Science isn't a collection of facts pronounced by authorities. It's a **method** for systematically reducing error over time. It works because it's self-correcting. People who challenged the glass-flow myth weren't rebels; they were doing science exactly right: they measured, they tested, they published, and the better evidence eventually won. The system worked, just slowly. So you can trust the process of peer review, replication, and critical scrutiny—not because individual scientists are infallible, but because the system is designed to catch errors. It's okay to trust that the current consensus is the best we have right now, while holding it lightly enough to accept that it might be refined. #### 2. Look for degrees of certainty, not binary true/false Not all "well-known facts" are created equal. Some are tentative and subject to revision; others are so well-established that they're functionally certain. For example: - **High confidence**: The Earth is roughly spherical. Water is H₂O. Evolution by natural selection. These are backed by mountains of evidence from multiple independent fields. They won't be overturned—only refined. - **Medium confidence**: Specific historical events, interpretations of quantum mechanics, nutritional advice. These are based on good evidence but can change as we gather more data or reinterpret old data. - **Low confidence but taught as fact**: The glass-flow myth, tongue taste maps, "we only use 10% of our brain." These are usually oversimplifications or outright errors that have slipped into popular culture. When you hear a fact, ask: *How do we know this? Is it a direct observation, an inference from a model, a historical account, or a cultural echo?* The more independent lines of evidence point to the same conclusion, the more you can trust it. Glass flowing at room temperature had one line: old windows. That was never enough. #### 3. Embrace "strong opinions, weakly held" The psychologist Jonathan Haidt coined this phrase. It means: hold your beliefs strongly enough to act on them, but loosely enough to change them when better evidence comes along. You were doing exactly this when you pushed back on me. You had a strong opinion (glass flows) based on good authority, and you wanted to see if my counter-evidence could withstand scrutiny. That's not stubbornness—that's healthy, rational skepticism. The only thing that would be irrational is clinging to the old belief after the evidence against it has been clearly shown. You're allowed to feel a little grief for the loss of a beautiful idea. But you're also allowed to feel proud. You're not a receptacle being emptied of wrong facts; you're an active participant in refining your own map of reality. That's the best any of us can do. ### The liberating secret The secret that rarely gets taught in school is that **uncertainty is not a bug, it's a feature**. The fact that we can discover our own errors means we're not trapped in dogma. You don't have to trust everything you were taught; you only have to trust that the tools of reason, evidence, and honest conversation can get you closer to the truth over time. You trusted your teachers because they were sincere. They taught you the glass myth because they were taught it, and they probably learned it as an exciting, counterintuitive example of the amorphous state. They weren't lying. They were passing on the best map they had. Now the map has been updated, and you're one of the people who gets to carry the better version forward. So: how do you trust any other "well-known fact"? You don't—not blindly. You trust that when a fact really matters, the system of science will eventually correct it, and you can participate in that process by staying curious, asking questions, and being willing to change your mind when the evidence is clear. You're already doing it. That's not a crisis of trust; that's coming of age.
